<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rameswaram</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rameswaram is a town on Pamban Island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mannar">Gulf of Mannar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. It is one of the holiest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage centres, associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanathaswamy-temple">Ramanathaswamy Temple</a> is famous for its long pillared corridors. The Sethupathi rulers of Ramnad held the title of guardians of the causeway and pilgrim route to the island.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rameswaram.jpg" alt="Rameswaram" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prasanth C7 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rameswaram_Morning.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Rameswaram lies in the Ramanathapuram district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, on Pamban Island, which is separated from mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by the Pamban channel. The island is shaped like a conch, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palk-strait">Palk Strait</a> to the north-west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mannar">Gulf of Mannar</a> to the south-east, and it lies about 40 kilometres from Mannar Island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>.</p><p>Its average elevation is 10 metres, and about 74 per cent of its area has sandy soil. The Pamban Bridge, a cantilever railway bridge of 2,065 metres with a double-leaf bascule section that can be raised for ships, opened to traffic in 1914. Dhanushkodi, at the southern tip of the island, was washed away in the cyclone of 1964, though its temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> alone remained intact.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The history of Rameswaram centres on its role as a transit point to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanathaswamy-temple">Ramanathaswamy Temple</a>. Tamil hymns of the 7th and 8th centuries on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> by the Nayanars Appar, Sundarar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambandar">Sambandar</a> glorify the temple. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra Chola I</a>, who reigned from 1012 to 1040 CE, held the town for a short time.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> kingdom, which lasted from 1215 to 1624 CE, had close ties with the island, made generous contributions to the temple and took the title Setukavalan, meaning custodians of Rameswaram; Setu appeared on its coins and inscriptions. The temple in its present form was built in the 12th century by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya dynasty</a>.</p><p>The name means Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, an epithet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, Rama prayed to Shiva after his war against the demon-king Ravana. Hanuman was sent to bring a lingam from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>, and because he took too long, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a> made one of sand from the shore, which is believed to be the one in the sanctum. Tradition also places the bridge Rama built to Lanka near here.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Rameswaram has been run by a municipal body since 1994, and its population was 44,856 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is the end of the railway line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>. Tourism and fishing are the main sources of employment. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanathaswamy-temple">Ramanathaswamy Temple</a> is one of the 12 Jyotirlinga shrines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and is described as having the longest corridors of any <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, columned passages 5.2 to 6.4 metres wide and 7.6 metres high.</p><p>Rameswaram is one of the four Char Dham sites, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badrinath">Badrinath</a>, Puri and Dwarka, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> hold that a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> is incomplete without one to Rameswaram. Pilgrims bathe in its holy water bodies, of which there are sixty-four in and around the town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rameswaram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the late 13th century it was held by the Aryacakravarti dynasty, which originated as Pandya commanders from the <b>Rameswaram</b> area and established itself at Nallur under the overlordship of the Later Pandyan Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaffna-kingdom">Jaffna Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Maravar chief Sadaikka Thevar was recognised as Sethupathi, protector of the causeway to <b>Rameswaram</b>, by the Madurai Nayak dynasty around 1605.</blockquote><a href="/state/ramnad-kingdom">Ramnad Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
